      "shortest": true,
      "solution": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main() {\n    char a[101], b[101], ans[101];\n\n    scanf(\"%100s\", a);\n    scanf(\"%100s\", b);\n\n    int n = strlen(a);\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        if (a[i] == b[i])\n            ans[i] = '=';\n        else if ((a[i] == 'Z' && b[i] == 'A') || b[i] == a[i] + 1)\n            ans[i] = '+';\n        else if ((a[i] == 'A' && b[i] == 'Z') || b[i] == a[i] - 1)\n            ans[i] = '-';\n        else\n            ans[i] = '?';\n    }\n\n    ans[n] = '\\0';\n\n    puts(ans);\n\n    return 0;\n}",
      "statement": "Compare two uppercase strings of equal length [[a]] and [[b]] character by character.\nOutput {{=}} if the two letters are the same.\nOutput {{+}} if the letter in [[b]] is the next letter of the alphabet after the letter in [[a]].\nOutput {{-}} if the letter in [[b]] is the previous letter of the alphabet before the letter in [[a]].\nOutput {{?}} if none of the above applies.\nThe alphabet loops in both directions:\n\nZ → A\nA → Z",
